WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.177
At its simplest, a model is a rule that maps an input to an output.

00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:10.647
If we already know the rule, we can write it directly.

00:00:10.647 --> 00:00:15.727
For example, if y equals two x plus one, the model is already known.

00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:19.197
Give me x, and I can calculate y.

00:00:19.197 --> 00:00:26.593
Machine learning becomes useful when the exact rule is unknown or too complicated to specify manually.

00:00:27.172 --> 00:00:34.569
Instead of writing every coefficient ourselves, we provide examples and let an algorithm estimate the relationship.

00:00:34.569 --> 00:00:41.569
That is why I want you to think of machine learning as learning a useful mathematical rule from data.

00:00:41.569 --> 00:00:45.384
The output of the learning process is still a model.

00:00:45.384 --> 00:00:49.584
The model takes features as input and produces a prediction.

00:00:49.584 --> 00:00:57.339
This basic structure appears again and again, whether the algorithm is linear regression, logistic regression,

00:00:57.339 --> 00:01:02.343
a decision tree, a neural network, or something much more complex.
